    {
      "id": "6f881d0d-1b4c-4873-9a3c-cebce961a986",
      "creator": "anonymous",
      "contributor": "anonymous",
      "projectId": "Basic_Example",
      "created": "2026-04-11T12:19:23.092Z",
      "modified": "2026-04-11T12:19:23.092Z",
      "name": "Interview 2",
      "color": "",
      "note": "",
      "date": "",
      "text": "Interviewer: Let’s go back, if you agree, to the moment when you were at the hospital lobby, waiting for news about your sister.\n\nInterviewee: When it was hard to breathe?\n\nInterviewer: Yes, take the time to let the moment come back to you … you were sitting in the lobby.\n\nInterviewee: (eyes closed)  I’m sitting in the hospital lobby, late in the afternoon. I’m waiting for news after my sister’s surgery.\n\nInterviewer: When you are sitting, what do you feel?\n\nInterviewee: I feel a heaviness in my stomach, like a cold stone that makes it hard to breathe fully. My shoulders are tense, my gaze fixed on the floor. (looks at the interviewer)\n\nInterviewer: What happened next? Your gaze was fixed on the floor…\n\nInterviewee: (eyes closed again) At one point, I hear a child’s laughter behind me. I notice my breathing lengthen slightly, the stone seems to crack. A faint warmth begins to rise along my chest. (hands move up)...\n\nInterviewer: a warmth rises\n\nInterviewer: Then, when the doctor appears at the end of the corridor with a reassuring smile, everything shifts: my shoulders loosen, my eyes fill with tears, and a wave of gentle warmth spreads through my whole body, as if the stone had turned into warm water. Interestingly, this is something I have experienced twice since, the first time was when…\n\nInterviewer: If I may interrupt you, this is interesting, but could we stick, if you agree, to what happened at this moment in this hospital? You say the stone had turned into warm water …\n\n(the interview continues)",
      "participantName": "",
      "isActive": true,
      "parentId": "Basic_Example",
